        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "<ul><li><p>Fox sinks winning putt on 18th for first major title</p></li><li><p>‘It’s going to be big back home in New Zealand’</p></li></ul><p>Ryan Fox has said he had no ­sensation in his hands as he hit the winning putt in the 154th Open Cham­pionship. Fox, who claimed <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/19/open-championship-final-round-ryan-fox-tommy-fleetwood-scottie-scheffler-rory-mcilroy-golf\">his first major title</a>, became the first New Zealander since Bob Charles in 1963 to win the Open. He did so in dramatic style, with a birdie putt on the final hole sufficient&nbsp;for a 68 and 10-under-par total which was enough to beat Cameron Young by one stroke.</p><p>Fox was typically brisk in his approach to playing the 18th. Reflecting on the moment of victory, though, he explained the state of his emotions. “You can’t feel your hands, I know that,” he said.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/19/ryan-fox-the-open-championship-2026-golf-new-zealand\">Continue reading...</a>"
